<BaseLayout title="Get your first 100 newsletter subscribers | Glenn Edley" description="A simple checklist for starting a newsletter: choose a free tool, create a signup page, share it on Instagram and welcome your first readers." canonicalPath="/first-100-subscribers/">
  <main>
    <span class="back"><a href="/articles-and-media/">← Articles &amp; Media</a></span>
    <h1>Your first 100 newsletter subscribers</h1>
    <p class="lead">You don’t need a huge following to start a newsletter. You need a clear reason to subscribe, a simple signup page and a personal invitation.</p>
    <p>I put this checklist together after a conversation in my Night Owls group. If you’ve been thinking about starting a newsletter, here’s how to get going.</p>
    <p class="aside">By Glenn Edley · Updated 20 October 2026</p>

    <div class="article-body">
      <section>
        <h2>1. Decide what people will get</h2>
        <p>Finish this sentence:</p>
        <blockquote><p>“I write a [weekly/fortnightly] email for [type of person] who wants to [outcome]. It includes [two or three useful things].”</p></blockquote>
        <p>Be specific. “One practical idea each week to help you find your first customers” gives someone a clearer reason to subscribe than “updates from me”.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>2. Pick a simple tool</h2>
        <p>I use and recommend Mailchimp in my work at <a href="https://spike.co.nz/">Spike</a>. For a simple newsletter starting from zero, these are two other options worth considering:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong><a href="https://sendfox.com/">SendFox</a>:</strong> Free for up to 1,000 contacts and 3,000 email sends a month. Includes one hosted signup page, one form and one automation for your welcome email.</li>
          <li><strong><a href="https://www.mailerlite.com/free-plan">MailerLite</a>:</strong> Another option with a visual email editor. Its free plan currently covers 250 subscribers and 2,500 monthly emails, with a landing page, signup forms and automations.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Both handle the basics in one place, without a separate website. My starting suggestion for this checklist is SendFox. Check the current plan limits before signing up.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>3. Create one signup page</h2>
        <p>Keep it simple:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A headline explaining what readers will get.</li>
          <li>A sentence about who it’s for and how often you’ll send.</li>
          <li>An email field, with first name optional.</li>
          <li>A clear button, such as “Send me the weekly ideas”.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>You can offer a checklist or guide if you already have something useful. You don’t need to create one before starting.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>4. Connect it to Instagram</h2>
        <p>Put the signup page link in your Instagram profile and give it a clear title, such as “Get my weekly ideas”.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Share a useful tip in a post or Reel and mention the newsletter.</li>
          <li>Share the signup link using a Story link sticker.</li>
          <li>Save the Story to a “Newsletter” Highlight.</li>
          <li>Pin an introduction post explaining what readers will get.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Send people straight to the signup page.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>5. Personally invite your first readers</h2>
        <p>Start with friends, family, colleagues and people you already talk to who might enjoy the subject. Something like:</p>
        <blockquote><p>“Hey [name], I’m starting a newsletter about [X, Y and Z]. I’m excited to share [specific benefit], and thought it might interest you. If you’d like to receive it, you can sign up here: [link].”</p></blockquote>
        <p>Make it personal. Let them choose to subscribe rather than adding their addresses yourself.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>6. Set up a welcome email</h2>
        <p>Keep this short too:</p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>Thanks for joining! Every [day/frequency], I’ll send you [promise].</p>
          <p>Here’s something useful to get you started: [tip or link].</p>
          <p>What’s your biggest question about [topic]? Hit reply and let me know.</p>
          <p>[Your name]</p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>In SendFox, connect an automation to the list collecting your signups and set the welcome email to send immediately. New accounts use a confirmation email, so readers need to confirm their subscription first.</p>
        <p>Follow the platform’s sender verification steps, then test the whole process with your own email address before sharing the link. <a href="https://help.sendfox.com/article/1089-start-a-newsletter">SendFox’s setup guide walks you through it.</a></p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>7. Work towards 100, a few people at a time</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Personally invite five relevant people a day.</li>
          <li>Share a useful sample from each issue on Instagram.</li>
          <li>Remind people what the next email will help them do.</li>
          <li>Ask readers to forward an issue to one person who would enjoy it. Include your signup link so that person can join.</li>
          <li>Share in relevant groups where invitations are welcome.</li>
          <li>Send your first issue even if only a handful of people have joined.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Your first goal is ten interested readers. Then send them something worth receiving.</strong></p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>A few useful reads</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://kk.org/thetechnium/1000-true-fans/">Kevin Kelly: 1,000 True Fans</a> explains why a relatively small, committed audience can matter. A true fan is more than an email subscriber, but the idea is a useful reminder that you don’t need to appeal to everyone.</li>
          <li><a href="https://spike.co.nz/the-email-marketing-framework/">Spike: The Email Marketing Framework</a> covers list growth, welcoming subscribers and keeping your emails relevant. Start with sections two and three.</li>
          <li><a href="https://help.sendfox.com/article/1089-start-a-newsletter">SendFox: How to start a newsletter</a> walks through the signup page, welcome email and first send.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

